[size=5][b]Crimson Desert Telemetry[/b][/size]
[b]Live light, player, camera and ambient data for Crimson Desert. See nearby lights on a 3D radar or as on-screen markers, and use the local API in your own tools.[/b]

Telemetry captures light contributions around you, including sources behind the camera. Physics queries can check for obstacles between the camera and those sources.

[b]Optional room lighting[/b] is available through the separate [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonHue/releases/tag/v2.0.0]CrimsonHue[/url] companion. It syncs room lighting through Hue, OpenRGB or Tuya LAN and offers a configurable menu color in the shared HUD. [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonHue/blob/v2.0.0/docs/USER-GUIDE.md]Lighting setup and requirements[/url].

[size=4][b]Telemetry features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]All-around light capture:[/b] current engine lights remain available beyond the camera view. Particle groups are summed, and one fire can still produce several light contributions.[/*]
[*][b]Light data:[/b] world position, linear HDR RGB, luminance, recognized spotlight direction/cone and renderer-selection metadata.[/*]
[*][b]Physics collision checks:[/b] clear, blocked or unknown, with ray counts and measurement age. Enabled by default.[/*]
[*][b]Three light feeds:[/b] raw contributions, grouped/smoothed contributions, and a clear-only grouped/smoothed feed. Raw records and RGB are preserved.[/*]
[*][b]Player and camera:[/b] player position/root orientation plus independent native render-camera position, orientation and projection.[/*]
[*][b]Ambient/sky:[/b] global sky RGB, optional camera-local sky visibility and a derived local ambient estimate. Local fields can be null.[/*]
[*][b]HUD and API:[/b] fullscreen markers, player-centered 3D radar, notices, local HTTP snapshots/health/schema and WebSocket streams.[/*]
[*][b]Independent activity feed:[/b] /v1/activity and /v1/activity/stream report acquisition state and separate simulation tick activity.[/*]
[*][b]SDR/HDR UI:[/b] SDR, HDR10 and scRGB support.[/*]
[/list]

[url=https://youtu.be/eyRkkTXAU64]Watch the telemetry and HUD demonstration[/url]
The video and screenshot show an earlier version of the telemetry HUD.
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/v2.2.5/media/screenshot1.jpg[/img]

[size=4][b]Important visibility limitation[/b][/size]
The mod casts nine physics rays from the [b]camera position[/b] toward points around each nearby source, including behind the camera.
[list]
[*][b]Clear:[/b] at least one sampled path is unobstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Blocked:[/b] all nine paths are obstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Unknown:[/b] missing, invalid, incomplete or older than 500 ms.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Visibility is estimated from the game's collision geometry.[/b] Dense cages and simplified collision hulls can mark visible lamps as blocked. Glass with collision can behave like an opaque surface, and geometry without suitable collision can leave a path clear.

By default, blocked markers are dimmed and unknown sources remain visible. [b]F11[/b] shows only fresh clear lights in both marker and radar views. The clear-only API feed excludes blocked and unknown sources before grouping.

The shared radius defaults to [b]100 game units around the player[/b], configurable through LightOverlay/Radius. Up to 256 targets share a bounded query budget; crowded scenes may refresh individual lights less often.

The raw and unfiltered smoothed feeds retain captured sources regardless of the collision result. The separate clear-only feed filters before grouping and smoothing.
[code]
GET /v1/lights/visible
WS  /v1/lights/visible/stream[/code]
If measurements are unavailable, the feed reports unavailable. Setting [SourceVisibility] Enabled=0 leaves raw data available but provides no measured clear-only output.

[size=4][b]Install or upgrade[/b][/size]
Requires:
[list]
[*]Windows x64 and Crimson Desert on Steam. Validated reference [b]build 26448710[/b] (EXE 1.0.0.3549).[/*]
[*]Microsoft [b].NET 8 ASP.NET Core Runtime (x64)[/b].[/*]
[*]One compatible x64 ASI loader installed for the game.[/*]
[/list]
[list]
[*]Close the game before installing, updating or removing the mod.[/*]
[*]Back up useful INI preferences. Disable/remove older Telemetry packages and any separate legacy console ASI.[/*]
[*]Download the ModManagers ZIP from the Files tab or the [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/releases/latest]latest GitHub release[/url]. Import the complete ZIP into your mod manager, then [b]activate and deploy[/b] it.[/*]
[*]Keep all six runtime files from the same version together beside the game EXE.[/*]
[*]Merge preferences into the supplied INI, launch the game and load a save.[/*]
[/list]
[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.asi
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.ini
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg[/code]

[b]DMM upgrade note[/b] If a previous installation leaves stale CDT DLL or CFG files behind, remove those leftovers with the game closed before deploying the new package. Preserve your ASI loader and backed-up INI. Keep the .cfg extensions to prevent the mod manager from treating the metadata as game patches.

Guarded automatic recovery can handle compatible game updates. Changes to the game's data layout can require a CDT update.

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]F8:[/b] show/hide telemetry HUD and 3D radar.[/*]
[*][b]F9:[/b] toggle details.[/*]
[*][b]F10:[/b] show/hide fullscreen light markers.[/*]
[*][b]F11:[/b] toggle complete / fresh-clear-only light views.[/*]
[/list]
Reassign F8-F11 in the INI using decimal Windows virtual-key codes. Use 0 to disable a shortcut. Restart the game to apply INI changes.

[size=4][b]Data and compatibility limits[/b][/size]
Authored, rendered and upstream light arrays can overlap. Treat them as separate feeds. The upstream feed reflects current renderer input and can omit lights. RGB values use linear HDR, and coverage varies across sky, emissive surfaces and effects. Fast motion can expose capture and projection latency.

The plugin uses guarded game hooks, GPU copies and physics queries. Live testing across HDR displays, frame generation and AMD or Intel configurations is limited.

